        {
          "problem": "Define\n\\[p = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}.\\]Find a way to write\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3}\\]in terms of $p$ and $q.$",
          "solution": "We count the number of times $\\frac{1}{n^3}$ appears in the sum\n\\[\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3},\\]where $n$ is a fixed positive integer.  (In other words, we are conditioning the sum on $j + k$.)  We get a term of $\\frac{1}{n^3}$ each time $j + k = n.$  The pairs $(j,k)$ that work are $(1,n - 1),$ $(2,n - 2),$ $\\dots,$ $(n - 1,1),$ for a total of $n - 1$ pairs.  Therefore,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} &= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{n - 1}{n^3} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{n}{n^3} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right) \\\\\n&= \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{p - q}.\n\\end{align*}",
          "answer": "p - q",
          "subject": "Intermediate Algebra",
          "level": 5,
          "unique_id": "test/intermediate_algebra/1994.json",
          "model_output": "We start by expanding the given sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\left(\\frac{1}{j^3} - \\frac{3}{j^2k} + \\frac{3}{jk^2} - \\frac{1}{k^3}\\right).\n\\]\n\nWe can separate the sums into three parts:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j^3} - 3 \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j^2k} + 3 \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{jk^2} - \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the formulas for the sums of reciprocals of squares and cubes, we have:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j^3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j^3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{3}{4},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j^2k} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j^2} \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1/2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2/3} = \\frac{3}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{jk^2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{j} \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1/2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2/3} = \\frac{3}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{3}{4}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values back into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\frac{3}{4} - 3 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} + 3 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{9}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} = \\boxed{0}.\n\\]",
          "model_answer": "0"
