          "level": 3,
          "unique_id": "test/intermediate_algebra/1000.json",
          "model_output": "Let $r_1,$ $r_2,$ and $r_3$ be the roots of the polynomial $x^3 + ax^2 + ax + 1 = 0.$  Then by Vieta's formulas, we have\n\\[r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -a,\\]\n\\[r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = a,\\]\nand\n\\[r_1r_2r_3 = -1.\\]\nSince all the roots are real, the discriminant of the cubic polynomial must be non-negative.  The discriminant is given by\n\\[\\Delta = 18a^3 - 4a^2 - 4a^3 - 27 = 14a^3 - 4a^2 - 27.\\]\nSetting $\\Delta \\ge 0,$ we have\n\\[14a^3 - 4a^2 - 27 \\ge 0.\\]\nBy inspection, we find that $a = 3$ is a solution.  To show that this is the smallest possible value of $a,$ we note that if $a < 3,$ then $14a^3 - 4a^2 - 27 < 0.$  Therefore, the smallest possible value of $a$ is $\\boxed{3}.$",
          "model_answer": "3"
