          "subject": "Algebra",
          "level": 5,
          "unique_id": "test/algebra/2193.json",
          "model_output": "To solve the equation $x = \\sqrt{11-2x} + 4$, we start by isolating the square root term:\n\n\\[ x - 4 = \\sqrt{11 - 2x} \\]\n\nNext, we square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[ (x - 4)^2 = 11 - 2x \\]\n\nExpanding the left side and simplifying:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 8x + 16 = 11 - 2x \\]\n\nRearranging terms to form a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 6x + 5 = 0 \\]\n\nFactoring the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ (x - 1)(x - 5) = 0 \\]\n\nThis gives us two possible solutions for $x$:\n\n\\[ x = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 5 \\]\n\nWe must verify these solutions in the original equation to ensure they are valid.\n\nFor $x = 1$:\n\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{11 - 2(1)} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{9} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 3 + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 7 \\]\n\nThis is not true, so $x = 1$ is not a valid solution.\n\nFor $x = 5$:\n\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{11 - 2(5)} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{1} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 1 + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 5 \\]\n\nThis is true, so $x = 5$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only value of $x$ that satisfies the equation is $\\boxed{5}$.",
