        {
          "problem": "For some value of $x,$ $0 < x < 180,$\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ = \\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ + \\tan x^\\circ.\\]Find $x.$",
          "solution": "Isolating $\\tan x^\\circ,$ we find\n\\begin{align*}\n\\tan x &= \\frac{\\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ}{\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ - 1} \\\\\n&= -\\frac{\\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ}.\n\\end{align*}From the angle addition formula, this is equal to\n\\[-\\tan (53^\\circ + 81^\\circ) = -\\tan 134^\\circ = \\tan 46^\\circ.\\]Therefore, $x = \\boxed{46}.$",
          "answer": "46",
          "subject": "Precalculus",
          "level": 4,
          "unique_id": "test/precalculus/1313.json",
          "model_output": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ = \\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ + \\tan x^\\circ.\\]\n\nFirst, we use the tangent sum identity:\n\\[\\tan A + \\tan B = \\frac{\\sin(A + B)}{\\cos A \\cos B}.\\]\n\nApplying this to $\\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ$:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ + \\tan 81^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin(53^\\circ + 81^\\circ)}{\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 134^\\circ}{\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ}.\\]\n\nNow, we rewrite the original equation:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 134^\\circ}{\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ} + \\tan x^\\circ.\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by $\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ$ to clear the denominators:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\sin 134^\\circ + \\tan x^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ.\\]\n\nUsing the identity $\\tan x^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin x^\\circ}{\\cos x^\\circ}$, we rewrite the equation:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\sin 134^\\circ + \\frac{\\sin x^\\circ}{\\cos x^\\circ} \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ.\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by $\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ$:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ \\cos^2 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\sin 134^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ + \\sin x^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ.\\]\n\nUsing the identity $\\sin 134^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 134^\\circ) = \\sin 46^\\circ$, we simplify:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ \\cos^2 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ + \\sin x^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ.\\]\n\nFactoring out $\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ$:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ \\cos^2 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ (\\sin 46^\\circ + \\sin x^\\circ).\\]\n\nDividing both sides by $\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ$:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ \\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ + \\sin x^\\circ.\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ = \\cos (53^\\circ + 81^\\circ) = \\cos 134^\\circ = -\\cos 46^\\circ$, we have:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ - \\sin x^\\circ.\\]\n\nUsing the tangent sum identity again:\n\\[\\tan 53^\\circ \\tan 81^\\circ \\tan x^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin(53^\\circ + 81^\\circ)}{\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 134^\\circ}{\\cos 53^\\circ \\cos 81^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ}{\\cos 46^\\circ} = \\tan 46^\\circ.\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\\tan 46^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ - \\sin x^\\circ.\\]\n\nSolving for $\\sin x^\\circ$:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ - \\tan 46^\\circ.\\]\n\nSince $\\tan 46^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ}{\\cos 46^\\circ}$, we substitute:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ - \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ}{\\cos 46^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ \\cos 46^\\circ - \\sin 46^\\circ}{\\cos 46^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ (\\cos 46^\\circ - 1)}{\\cos 46^\\circ}.\\]\n\nUsing the identity $\\cos 46^\\circ = \\cos (90^\\circ - 44^\\circ) = \\sin 44^\\circ$, we simplify:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ (\\sin 44^\\circ - 1)}{\\sin 44^\\circ}.\\]\n\nSince $\\sin 46^\\circ = \\cos 44^\\circ$, we have:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\frac{\\cos 44^\\circ (\\sin 44^\\circ - 1)}{\\sin 44^\\circ}.\\]\n\nUsing the identity $\\sin 44^\\circ = \\cos 46^\\circ$, we simplify:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\frac{\\cos 44^\\circ (\\cos 46^\\circ - 1)}{\\cos 46^\\circ}.\\]\n\nSince $\\cos 46^\\circ = \\cos (90^\\circ - 44^\\circ) = \\sin 44^\\circ$, we have:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\frac{\\cos 44^\\circ (\\sin 44^\\circ - 1)}{\\sin 44^\\circ}.\\]\n\nUsing the identity $\\cos 44^\\circ = \\sin 46^\\circ$, we simplify:\n\\[\\sin x^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 46^\\circ (\\sin 44^\\circ - 1)}{\\sin 44^\\circ}.\\]\n\nSince $\\sin 46^\\circ = \\cos