          "unique_id": "test/algebra/2058.json",
          "model_output": "To simplify the expression $\\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{3}}$, we can multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $2-\\sqrt{3}$. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{2-\\sqrt{3}}{2-\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{(1+\\sqrt{2})(2-\\sqrt{3})}{(2+\\sqrt{3})(2-\\sqrt{3})}\n\\]\n\nExpanding the numerator and denominator, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}}{4 - 3} = \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}}{1}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying further, we have:\n\n\\[\n2 - \\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten in the form $A(1+\\sqrt{B})-(\\sqrt{C}+\\sqrt{D})$ as:\n\n\\[\n2 - \\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{6} = 2(1 + \\sqrt{2}) - (\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{6})\n\\]\n\nThus, $A=2$, $B=2$, $C=3$, and $D=6$. Therefore, $A+B+C+D=2+2+3+6=\\boxed{13}$.",
