        {
          "problem": "Let $(a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n)$ be a sequence of positive real numbers, such that\n\\[\\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i = 96, \\quad \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 = 144, \\quad \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 = 216.\\]Find the sum of all possible values of $n.$",
          "solution": "By Cauchy-Schwarz,\n\\[(a_1 + a_2 + \\dots + a_n)(a_1^3 + a_2^3 + \\dots + a_n^3) \\ge (a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \\dots + a_n^2)^2.\\]Since $96 \\cdot 216 = 144^2,$ we have equality in the Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality, which means\n\\[\\frac{a_1^3}{a_1} = \\frac{a_2^3}{a_2} = \\dots = \\frac{a_n^3}{a_n}.\\]Then $a_1^2 = a_2^2 = \\dots = a_n^2,$ so $a_1 = a_2 = \\dots = a_n.$\n\nFrom the given, $na_1 = 96$ and $na_1^2 = 144.$  Dividing these equations, we get $a_1 = \\frac{3}{2},$ so $n = \\boxed{64}.$",
          "answer": "64",
          "subject": "Intermediate Algebra",
          "level": 5,
          "unique_id": "test/intermediate_algebra/2022.json",
          "model_output": "Let $S = \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i = 96,$ $Q = \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 = 144,$ and $R = \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 = 216.$\n\nWe have:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS^2 &= \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 + 2Q \\\\\n&= 144 + 2 \\cdot 144 \\\\\n&= 288.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, $S = \\sqrt{288} = 12\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, we use the inequality:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i\\right)^3 &= \\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 + 3\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\\\\n&= R + 3\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\\\\n&= 216 + 3\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} a_i a_j (a_i + a_j) \\\\\n&= 216 + 3\\left(\\frac{S^2}{Q}\\right) \\\\\n&= 216 + 3\\left(\\frac{288}{144}\\right) \\\\\n&= 216 + 6 \\\\\n&= 222.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, $\\left(\\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i\\right)^3 = 222,$ so $\\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i = \\sqrt[3]{222} = 6\\sqrt[3]{37}.$\n\nSince $S = 12\\sqrt{2} = 6\\sqrt{2} \\cdot 2,$ we have $n = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the sum of all possible values of $n$ is $\\boxed{2}.$",
          "model_answer": "2"
        },
        {
          "problem": "Express the quotient $413_5 \\div 2_5$ in base 5.",
          "solution": "We may carry out long division in base 5 just as in base 10. We have  \\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{2} & 0 & 4 \\\\\n\\cline{2-4}\n2 & 4 & 1 & 3 \\\\\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{4} & \\downarrow & \\\\ \\cline{2-2}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 1 & \\\\\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & \\downarrow \\\\ \\cline{3-3}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 3 \\\\\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\cline{3-4}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 0\n\\end{array}\n\\]for a quotient of $\\boxed{204_5}$. Note that in the above calculation we have used that $13_5$ divided by $2_5$ is $4_5$, which follows from $4_5\\times2_5=8_{10}=13_5$.",
          "answer": "204_5",
