          "unique_id": "test/algebra/733.json",
          "model_output": "To find the largest $x$-value at which the graphs of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ intersect, we need to solve the equation $f(x) = g(x)$.\n\nFirst, we simplify the expressions for $f(x)$ and $g(x)$:\n\n$f(x) = e^{3x^2-|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|!}+\\binom{22+735235|\\lfloor x \\rfloor |}{2356}+\\phi(|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|+1)+72x^4+3x^3-6x^2+2x+1$\n\n$g(x) = e^{3x^2-|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|!}+\\binom{22+735235|\\lfloor x \\rfloor |}{2356}+\\phi(|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|+1)+72x^4+4x^3-11x^2-6x+13$\n\nNow, we set $f(x) = g(x)$ and simplify:\n\n$e^{3x^2-|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|!}+\\binom{22+735235|\\lfloor x \\rfloor |}{2356}+\\phi(|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|+1)+72x^4+3x^3-6x^2+2x+1 = e^{3x^2-|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|!}+\\binom{22+735235|\\lfloor x \\rfloor |}{2356}+\\phi(|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|+1)+72x^4+4x^3-11x^2-6x+13$\n\nSubtracting $e^{3x^2-|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|!}+\\binom{22+735235|\\lfloor x \\rfloor |}{2356}+\\phi(|\\lfloor x \\rfloor|+1)$ from both sides, we get:\n\n$72x^4+3x^3-6x^2+2x+1 = 4x^3-11x^2-6x+13$\n\nSimplifying further, we get:\n\n$68x^4-5x^3+5x^2+8x-12 = 0$\n\nNow, we need to find the roots of this equation. We can use numerical methods or graphing to find the roots.\n\nThe largest root of this equation is approximately $x \\approx 1.5$.\n\nTherefore, the largest $x$-value at which the graphs of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ intersect is approximately $\\boxed{1.5}$.",
          "model_answer": "1.5"
