          "level": 4,
          "unique_id": "test/algebra/1578.json",
          "model_output": "Let's denote Amy's age as \\( A \\), Ben's age as \\( B \\), and Chris's age as \\( C \\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have the following equations:\n\n1. The average of their ages is 6:\n\\[\n\\frac{A + B + C}{3} = 6\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\nA + B + C = 18\n\\]\n\n2. Four years ago, Chris was the same age as Amy is now:\n\\[\nC - 4 = A\n\\]\n\n3. In four years, Ben's age will be \\(\\frac{3}{5}\\) of Amy's age at that time:\n\\[\nB + 4 = \\frac{3}{5}(A + 4)\n\\]\n\nNow, we have a system of three equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nA + B + C = 18 \\\\\nC - 4 = A \\\\\nB + 4 = \\frac{3}{5}(A + 4)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we can express \\( A \\) in terms of \\( C \\):\n\\[\nA = C - 4\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( A = C - 4 \\) into the first equation:\n\\[\n(C - 4) + B + C = 18\n\\]\nSimplify:\n\\[\n2C + B - 4 = 18\n\\]\n\\[\n2C + B = 22\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( A = C - 4 \\) into the third equation:\n\\[\nB + 4 = \\frac{3}{5}(C - 4 + 4)\n\\]\nSimplify:\n\\[\nB + 4 = \\frac{3}{5}C\n\\]\n\\[\nB = \\frac{3}{5}C - 4\n\\]\n\nNow we have two expressions for \\( B \\):\n\\[\n2C + B = 22\n\\]\n\\[\nB = \\frac{3}{5}C - 4\n\\]\n\nSet the two expressions for \\( B \\) equal to each other:\n\\[\n2C + \\frac{3}{5}C - 4 = 22\n\\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n2C + \\frac{3}{5}C = 26\n\\]\nConvert \\( 2C \\) to a fraction with a denominator of 5:\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{5}C + \\frac{3}{5}C = 26\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{5}C = 26\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by \\(\\frac{5}{13}\\):\n\\[\nC = 26 \\times \\frac{5}{13}\n\\]\n\\[\nC = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, Chris is $\\boxed{10}$ years old.",
