          "model_output": "Given that $\\omega^{1997} = 1$ and $\\omega \\neq 1$, we can use the fact that $\\omega$ is a primitive 1997th root of unity. This means that $\\omega^k$ for $k = 1, 2, \\dots, 1997$ are all distinct roots of the equation $x^{1997} - 1 = 0$.\n\nWe can rewrite the given expression as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega} + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^2} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{1997}}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that each term in the sum can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^k} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\omega^k} = \\frac{1 - \\omega^k}{1 - \\omega^k - 1 + \\omega^k} = \\frac{1 - \\omega^k}{- \\omega^k}.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 - \\omega^k}{- \\omega^k} = \\frac{1}{\\omega^k} - 1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{1997} \\left( \\frac{1}{\\omega^k} - 1 \\right).\n\\]\n\nThis is a telescoping series, where most terms cancel out. Writing out the first few terms, we get:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{\\omega} - 1 \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{\\omega^2} - 1 \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{\\omega^3} - 1 \\right) + \\dots + \\left( \\frac{1}{\\omega^{1997}} - 1 \\right).\n\\]\n\nAll terms except the first and last cancel out, leaving:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\omega} + \\frac{1}{\\omega^2} + \\frac{1}{\\omega^3} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{\\omega^{1997}} - 1997.\n\\]\n\nSince $\\omega$ is a primitive 1997th root of unity, $\\omega^k$ for $k = 1, 2, \\dots, 1997$ are all distinct. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\omega^k}$ is also distinct for $k = 1, 2, \\dots, 1997$.\n\nThus, the sum simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\omega} + \\frac{1}{\\omega^2} + \\frac{1}{\\omega^3} + \\dots + \\frac{1}{\\omega^{1997}} - 1997.\n\\]\n\nSince $\\omega$ is a primitive 1997th root of unity, $\\omega^k$ for $k = 1, 2, \\dots, 1997$ are all distinct. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{\\omega^k}$ is also distinct for $k = 1, 2, \\dots, 1997$.\n\nThus, the sum simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\omega} + \\frac{1}{\\omega^2} + \\frac{1}{\\omega^3} + \\dots