        {
          "problem": "Given\n\\[\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix},\\]compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}.$",
          "solution": "Note that\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\]and\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} = -\\mathbf{I}.\\]Then\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} &= (\\mathbf{A}^3)^9 + (\\mathbf{A}^3)^{10} \\mathbf{A} + (\\mathbf{A}^3)^{13} \\mathbf{A} \\\\\n&= (-\\mathbf{I})^9 + (-\\mathbf{I})^{10} \\mathbf{A} + (-\\mathbf{I})^{13} \\mathbf{A} \\\\\n&= -\\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{A} \\\\\n&= -\\mathbf{I} = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\end{align*}",
          "answer": "\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}",
          "subject": "Precalculus",
          "level": 4,
          "unique_id": "test/precalculus/920.json",
          "model_output": "To solve the problem, we first need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0$, where $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue and $\\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix.\n\nFor $\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, the characteristic equation is:\n\\[\\det\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) - 7(-1) = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 1 = 0.\\]\n\nSolving this quadratic equation, we find the eigenvalues:\n\\[\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\]\n\nNext, we find the corresponding eigenvectors. Let $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFor $\\lambda = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}$, we have:\n\\[\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nThis implies $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFor $\\lambda = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{5}}{2}$, we have:\n\\[\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nThis implies $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFor $\\lambda = 1$, we have:\n\\[\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nThis implies $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFor $\\lambda = -1$, we have:\n\\[\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\nThis implies $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nNow, we can express $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$ in terms of the eigenvalues and eigenvectors:\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\lambda_1^{27} \\mathbf{v}_1 + \\lambda_2^{27} \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\lambda_1^{40} \\mathbf{v}_1 + \\lambda_2^{40} \\mathbf{v}_2.\\]\n\nUsing the fact that $\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2 = 1$ and $\\lambda_1 \\lambda_2 = -1$, we can simplify the expression:\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = (\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2)^{27} \\mathbf{v}_1 + (\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2)^{40} \\mathbf{v}_1 + (\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2)^{27} \\mathbf{v}_2 + (\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2)^{40} \\mathbf{v}_2.\\]\n\nSince $\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2 = 1$, we have:\n\\[\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2.\\]\n\nNow, we can find the sum of the eigenvectors:\n\\[\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}}.\\]",
          "model_answer": "\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}"
        },
