          "unique_id": "test/counting_and_probability/894.json",
          "model_output": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the different ways the cars can stack up in the three lanes. We can break this down into cases based on the number of cars in the left-turn lane.\n\n1. **Case 1: No cars in the left-turn lane.**\n   - There are 3! ways to arrange the cars in the straight-going and right-turn lanes.\n   - Total ways for this case: \\(3! = 6\\).\n\n2. **Case 2: One car in the left-turn lane.**\n   - There are 3 ways to choose which car goes in the left-turn lane.\n   - The remaining 5 cars can be arranged in the remaining 5 lanes in \\(5!\\) ways.\n   - Total ways for this case: \\(3 \\times 5! = 3 \\times 120 = 360\\).\n\n3. **Case 3: Two cars in the left-turn lane.**\n   - There are 3 ways to choose which two cars go in the left-turn lane.\n   - The remaining 4 cars can be arranged in the remaining 4 lanes in \\(4!\\) ways.\n   - Total ways for this case: \\(3 \\times 4! = 3 \\times 24 = 72\\).\n\n4. **Case 4: Three cars in the left-turn lane.**\n   - There is only 1 way to choose which three cars go in the left-turn lane.\n   - The remaining 3 cars can be arranged in the remaining 3 lanes in \\(3!\\) ways.\n   - Total ways for this case: \\(1 \\times 3! = 1 \\times 6 = 6\\).\n\nAdding up all the cases, we get the total number of ways the cars can stack up:\n\\[6 + 360 + 72 + 6 = \\boxed{438}\\]",
          "model_answer": "438"
