          "model_output": "Since the two lines are parallel, we can find the distance between them by finding the distance between a point on each line.\n\nLet's choose a point on the first line, $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, and a point on the second line, $\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} + u \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThe direction vectors of the two lines are $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$, respectively.\n\nThe distance between two parallel lines is given by the formula:\n\\[ \\text{distance} = \\frac{\\| \\mathbf{d} \\times \\mathbf{n} \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{n} \\|} \\]\nwhere $\\mathbf{d}$ is the direction vector of one line, and $\\mathbf{n}$ is the direction vector of the other line.\n\nFirst, we find the cross product of $\\mathbf{d}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$:\n\\[ \\mathbf{d} \\times \\mathbf{n} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} (-9)(-4) - (-2)(-18) \\\\ (-2)(4) - (2)(-4) \\\\ (2)(-18) - (-9)(4) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 18 - 36 \\\\ -8 + 8 \\\\ -36 + 36 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nThe magnitude of the cross product is:\n\\[ \\| \\mathbf{d} \\times \\mathbf{n} \\| = \\sqrt{(-18)^2 + 0^2 + 0^2} = 18 \\]\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of the direction vector $\\mathbf{n}$:\n\\[ \\| \\mathbf{n} \\| = \\sqrt{4^2 + (-18)^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 324 + 16} = \\sqrt{356} \\]\n\nFinally, we calculate the distance between the two lines:\n\\[ \\text{distance} = \\frac{\\| \\mathbf{d} \\times \\mathbf{n} \\|}{\\| \\mathbf{n} \\|} = \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{356}} = \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 89}} = \\frac{18}{2\\sqrt{89}} = \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{89}} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{89}}{89} \\]\n\nSo, the distance between the two parallel lines is $\\boxed{\\fr