          "unique_id": "test/algebra/2486.json",
          "model_output": "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of $p$ that satisfy both inequalities.\n\nFirst, let's solve the inequality $0\\ge 54p-144$.\n\nAdd 144 to both sides:\n\\[ 144 \\ge 54p \\]\n\nDivide both sides by 54:\n\\[ \\frac{144}{54} \\ge p \\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ \\frac{24}{9} \\ge p \\]\n\n\\[ \\frac{8}{3} \\ge p \\]\n\nNow, let's solve the inequality $0>12-20p$.\n\nAdd 20p to both sides:\n\\[ 20p > 12 \\]\n\nDivide both sides by 20:\n\\[ p > \\frac{12}{20} \\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[ p > \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nNow, we have two inequalities:\n\\[ \\frac{8}{3} \\ge p \\]\n\\[ p > \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nTo find the values of $p$ that satisfy both inequalities, we need to find the intersection of the two intervals.\n\nThe intersection of the two intervals is:\n\\[ \\frac{8}{3} \\ge p \\]\n\\[ p > \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nSince $\\frac{8}{3} \\approx 2.67$ and $\\frac{3}{5} = 0.6$, the intersection of the two intervals is:\n\\[ p \\in \\left[\\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right] \\]\n\nTherefore, the solution to the problem is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\left[\\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right]} \\]",
