          "model_output": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1}\\) for \\(x > 0\\), we start by simplifying the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + 5)(x + 2)}{x + 1} = \\frac{x^2 + 7x + 10}{x + 1}\n\\]\n\nNext, we perform polynomial long division on \\(x^2 + 7x + 10\\) by \\(x + 1\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cccc}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{x} & +6 \\\\\n\\cline{2-5}\nx + 1 & x^2 & +7x & +10 \\\\\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^2} & +x \\\\\n\\cline{2-3}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & +6x & +10 \\\\\n\\cline{3-5}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & +6x & +6 \\\\\n\\cline{4-5}\n\\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & +4 & \\\\\n\\cline{5-5}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 7x + 10}{x + 1} = x + 6 + \\frac{4}{x + 1}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to minimize the expression \\(x + 6 + \\frac{4}{x + 1}\\) for \\(x > 0\\). To do this, we find the critical points by taking the derivative of the function:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = x + 6 + \\frac{4}{x + 1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf'(x) = 1 - \\frac{4}{(x + 1)^2}\n\\]\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero to find the critical points:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{4}{(x + 1)^2} = 0\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{(x + 1)^2} = 1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(x + 1)^2 = 4\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx + 1 = 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = -2\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x > 0\\), we discard \\(x + 1 = -2\\) and solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx + 1 = 2 \\implies x = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, we evaluate the function at \\(x = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nf(1) = 1 + 6 + \\frac{4}{1 + 1} = 1 + 6 + 2 = 9\n\\]\n\nTo confirm that this is a minimum, we check the second derivative:\n\n\\[\nf''(x) = \\frac{8}{(x + 1)^3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(f''(x) > 0\\) for all \\(x > 0\\), the function is concave up at \\(x = 1\\), indicating a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of the expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
