          "problem": "A Senate committee has 8 Republicans and 6 Democrats.  In how many ways can we form a subcommittee of 5 members that has at least one member from each party?",
          "solution": "There are a total of $\\binom{14}{5}=2002$ ways of selecting a subcommittee of 5 with no restrictions on the membership. Of these committees, the only ones that will violate the given condition are the ones that consist entirely of Republicans or entirely of Democrats. There are $\\binom{8}{5}=56$ possible subcommittees that have all 5 members selected from among the 8 Republicans and $\\binom{6}{5}=6$ possible subcommittees that have all 5 members selected from among the 6 Democrats. Subtracting the number of subcommittees that don't work from the total number of possible subcommittees gives us our answer: $2002-56-6=\\boxed{1940}$.",
          "answer": "1940",
          "subject": "Counting & Probability",
          "level": 5,
          "unique_id": "test/counting_and_probability/216.json",
          "model_output": "To form a subcommittee of 5 members with at least one member from each party, we can use the principle of complementary counting. First, we calculate the total number of ways to form a subcommittee of 5 members from the 14 available members (8 Republicans and 6 Democrats). This is given by the combination formula $\\binom{14}{5}$.\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to form a subcommittee that does not meet the criteria (i.e., all members are from the same party). There are two such cases: all 5 members are from the Republicans or all 5 members are from the Democrats.\n\nFor all 5 members from the Republicans, we choose 5 out of the 8 available Republicans. This is given by $\\binom{8}{5}$.\n\nFor all 5 members from the Democrats, we choose 5 out of the 6 available Democrats. This is given by $\\binom{6}{5}$.\n\nThe total number of ways to form a subcommittee that does not meet the criteria is the sum of these two cases: $\\binom{8}{5} + \\binom{6}{5}$.\n\nNow, we subtract the number of ways to form a subcommittee that does not meet the criteria from the total number of ways to form a subcommittee of 5 members from the 14 available members:\n\n$$\\binom{14}{5} - (\\binom{8}{5} + \\binom{6}{5})$$\n\nCalculating the values:\n\n$$\\binom{14}{5} = 2002$$\n$$\\binom{8}{5} = 56$$\n$$\\binom{6}{5} = 6$$\n\nSo, the number of ways to form a subcommittee of 5 members with at least one member from each party is:\n\n$$2002 - (56 + 6) = 2002 - 62 = 1940$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\boxed{1940}$.",
          "model_answer": "1940"
