          "model_output": "To solve for the positive value of $x$ such that $\\sqrt[3]{x^2 - 4x + 4} = 16$, we start by cubing both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{x^2 - 4x + 4} = 16\n\\]\n\nCubing both sides gives:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 4 = 16^3\n\\]\n\nCalculating $16^3$:\n\n\\[\n16^3 = 4096\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 4 = 4096\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 4096 from both sides to set the equation to zero:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x + 4 - 4096 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 4088 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in the form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$, where $a = 1$, $b = -4$, and $c = -4088$. We can solve this using the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of $a$, $b$, and $c$:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-(-4) \\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-4088)}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 16352}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16368}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm 127.5}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions for $x$:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 + 127.5}{2} = \\frac{131.5}{2} = 65.75\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 - 127.5}{2} = \\frac{-123.5}{2} = -61.75\n\\]\n\nSince we are looking for the positive value of $x$, we discard the negative solution:\n\n\\[\nx = 65.75\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of $x$ that satisfies the equation is $\\boxed{65.75}$.",
