          "unique_id": "test/number_theory/427.json",
          "model_output": "To find the sum of the distinct prime divisors of $A$, where $A$ is the sum of the positive divisors of $500$, we first need to determine the prime factorization of $500$.\n\nThe prime factorization of $500$ is $2^2 \\cdot 5^3$.\n\nThe sum of the divisors of a number can be found using the formula for the sum of divisors of a number based on its prime factorization. For a number $n = p_1^{e_1} \\cdot p_2^{e_2} \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot p_k^{e_k}$, the sum of the divisors is given by:\n\n\\[\n(1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\ldots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\ldots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\ldots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nApplying this formula to $500 = 2^2 \\cdot 5^3$, we get:\n\n\\[\n(1 + 2 + 2^2)(1 + 5 + 5^2 + 5^3)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n\\[\n(1 + 2 + 4) = 7 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (1 + 5 + 25 + 125) = 156\n\\]\n\nMultiplying these results together:\n\n\\[\n7 \\cdot 156 = 1092\n\\]\n\nThus, $A = 1092$.\n\nNow, we need to find the distinct prime divisors of $1092$. We factorize $1092$ as follows:\n\n\\[\n1092 = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\nThe distinct prime divisors of $1092$ are $2$, $3$, $7$, and $13$.\n\nFinally, we sum these distinct prime divisors:\n\n\\[\n2 + 3 + 7 + 13 = 25\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the distinct prime divisors of $A$ is $\\boxed{25}$.",
