        {
          "problem": "Consider the function $z(x,y)$ describing the paraboloid\n\\[z = (2x - y)^2 - 2y^2 - 3y.\\]Archimedes and Brahmagupta are playing a game.  Archimedes first chooses $x.$  Afterwards, Brahmagupta chooses $y.$  Archimedes wishes to minimize $z$ while Brahmagupta wishes to maximize $z.$  Assuming that Brahmagupta will play optimally, what value of $x$ should Archimedes choose?",
          "solution": "Expanding $z,$ we get\n\\begin{align*}\nz &= 4x^2 - 4xy + y^2 - 2y^2 - 3y \\\\\n&= -y^2 - (4x + 3) y + 4x^2.\n\\end{align*}After Archimedes chooses $x,$ Brahmagupta will choose\n\\[y = -\\frac{4x + 3}{2}\\]in order to maximize $z.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\nz &= -\\left( -\\frac{4x + 3}{2} \\right)^2 - (4x + 3) \\left( -\\frac{4x + 3}{2} \\right)^2 + 4x^2 \\\\\n&= 8x^2 + 6x + \\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{align*}To minimize this expression, Archimedes should choose $x = -\\frac{6}{16} = \\boxed{-\\frac{3}{8}}.$",
          "answer": "-\\frac{3}{8}",
          "subject": "Intermediate Algebra",
          "level": 5,
          "unique_id": "test/intermediate_algebra/1791.json",
          "model_output": "To find the value of $x$ that minimizes $z$ while Brahmagupta chooses $y$ to maximize $z$, we start by differentiating $z$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ to find the critical points.\n\nFirst, we differentiate $z$ with respect to $x$:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\frac{d}{dx} [(2x - y)^2 - 2y^2 - 3y] = 4(2x - y) - 4y = 8x - 8y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we differentiate $z$ with respect to $y$:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\frac{d}{dy} [(2x - y)^2 - 2y^2 - 3y] = -4(2x - y) - 4y = -8x + 8y.\n\\]\n\nTo find the critical points, we set the partial derivatives equal to zero:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n8x - 8y = 0 \\\\\n-8x + 8y = 0\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations simultaneously, we find:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n8x = 8y \\\\\n-8x = -8y\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThis implies $x = y$.\n\nNow, we substitute $x = y$ into the original equation for $z$:\n\\[\nz = (2x - x)^2 - 2x^2 - 3x = x^2 - 2x^2 - 3x = -x^2 - 3x.\n\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value of $z$, we complete the square:\n\\[\nz = -x^2 - 3x = -(x^2 + 3x) = -(x^2 + 3x + \\frac{9}{4}) + \\frac{9}{4} = -(x + \\frac{3}{2})^2 + \\frac{9}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThe minimum value of $z$ occurs when $(x + \\frac{3}{2})^2 = 0$, which gives $x = -\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, Archimedes should choose $x = \\boxed{-\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
