          "unique_id": "test/counting_and_probability/761.json",
          "model_output": "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. Let's define the following sets:\n\n- \\( A \\) is the set of boxes containing pencils.\n- \\( B \\) is the set of boxes containing pens.\n- \\( A \\cap B \\) is the set of boxes containing both pencils and pens.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- \\( |A| = 5 \\) (the number of boxes containing pencils)\n- \\( |B| = 4 \\) (the number of boxes containing pens)\n- \\( |A \\cap B| = 2 \\) (the number of boxes containing both pencils and pens)\n\nWe need to find the number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils, which is the total number of boxes minus the number of boxes containing either pencils or pens or both.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of boxes containing either pencils or pens or both:\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = 5 + 4 - 2 \\]\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = 7 \\]\n\nNow, we subtract the number of boxes containing either pencils or pens or both from the total number of boxes to find the number of boxes containing neither pens nor pencils:\n\\[ \\text{Number of boxes containing neither pens nor pencils} = 10 - |A \\cup B| \\]\n\\[ \\text{Number of boxes containing neither pens nor pencils} = 10 - 7 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Number of boxes containing neither pens nor pencils} = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
