          "unique_id": "test/algebra/1960.json",
          "model_output": "To find the value of $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$, we need to find the inverse function of $f(x)$ and then evaluate it at $\\frac{1}{5}$.\n\nGiven $f(x) = \\frac{2}{x+1}$, we want to find $f^{-1}(x)$. To do this, we start by setting $y = f(x)$ and solving for $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\n\\[ y = \\frac{2}{x+1} \\]\n\nTo isolate $x$, we multiply both sides by $x+1$ and then subtract $y$ from both sides:\n\n\\[ y(x+1) = 2 \\]\n\\[ yx + y = 2 \\]\n\\[ yx = 2 - y \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{2 - y}{y} \\]\n\nNow, we can express $x$ in terms of $y$ as:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{2 - y}{y} \\]\n\nThis is the inverse function of $f(x)$. To find $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$, we substitute $\\frac{1}{5}$ for $y$ in the inverse function:\n\n\\[ f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) = \\frac{2 - \\frac{1}{5}}{\\frac{1}{5}} \\]\n\nSimplify the expression:\n\n\\[ f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) = \\frac{\\frac{10}{5} - \\frac{1}{5}}{\\frac{1}{5}} \\]\n\\[ f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) = \\frac{\\frac{9}{5}}{\\frac{1}{5}} \\]\n\\[ f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) = 9 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$ is $\\boxed{9}$.",
