          "model_output": "1. **Define the problem and the probability**: We need to find the number of fair 6-sided dice, $n$, such that the probability of exactly two dice showing a number other than 1 is $\\frac{25}{216}$.\n\n2. **Calculate the probability of a single die showing a number other than 1**:\n   - A fair 6-sided die has 6 possible outcomes: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.\n   - The probability of a single die showing a number other than 1 is $\\frac{5}{6}$.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability of exactly two dice showing a number other than 1**:\n   - The number of ways to choose 2 dice out of $n$ dice is given by the binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{2}$.\n   - The probability of exactly two dice showing a number other than 1 is $\\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2}$.\n\n4. **Set up the equation and solve for $n$**:\n   - We are given that the probability of exactly two dice showing a number other than 1 is $\\frac{25}{216}$.\n   - Therefore, we have the equation:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{25}{216}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the equation:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{25}{216}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{216}{25}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{216}{25} = 1\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = 1\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac{2}{5}$:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Simplify further:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{2}{5}\n     \\]\n   - Multiply both sides by $\\frac