          "unique_id": "test/counting_and_probability/731.json",
          "model_output": "1. **Define the Problem**: We need to find the probability that a randomly chosen point inside rectangle $ABCD$ is closer to the center $O$ than to any of the vertices $A$, $B$, $C$, or $D$.\n\n2. **Symmetry and Distance Calculation**:\n   - Let $P$ be a point inside the rectangle.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $O$ is $d$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $A$ is $d_A$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $B$ is $d_B$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $C$ is $d_C$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $D$ is $d_D$.\n\n3. **Distance from $P$ to the Center**:\n   - The distance from $P$ to $O$ is $d$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $A$ is $d_A$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $B$ is $d_B$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $C$ is $d_C$.\n   - The distance from $P$ to $D$ is $d_D$.\n\n4. **Condition for Closeness**:\n   - For $P$ to be closer to $O$ than to any vertex, we need $d < d_A$, $d < d_B$, $d < d_C$, and $d < d_D$.\n\n5. **Symmetry and Probability**:\n   - Due to the symmetry of the rectangle, the probability that $P$ is closer to $O$ than to any vertex is the same as the probability that $P$ is closer to $O$ than to any other point inside the rectangle.\n   - The probability that $P$ is closer to $O$ than to any other point inside the rectangle is $\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - The probability that a randomly chosen point inside the rectangle is closer to the center $O$ than to any of the vertices is $\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]",
