          "model_output": "Let's denote the number of students who showed up for the archery class this week as \\( n \\). According to the problem, \\( n \\) must be a number between 30 and 50, inclusive.\n\nThe problem states that if both Betty and Wilma join the students, they still cannot break the students into groups of equal size. This means that \\( n \\) must be such that it is not divisible by 3. In other words, \\( n \\) must leave a remainder of 1 when divided by 3.\n\nTo find the possible values of \\( n \\), we need to identify all numbers between 30 and 50 that are not divisible by 3. We can do this by checking each number in the range:\n\n- 30: \\( 30 \\div 3 = 10 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 31: \\( 31 \\div 3 = 10 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 32: \\( 32 \\div 3 = 10 \\) (remainder 2)\n- 33: \\( 33 \\div 3 = 11 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 34: \\( 34 \\div 3 = 11 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 35: \\( 35 \\div 3 = 11 \\) (remainder 2)\n- 36: \\( 36 \\div 3 = 12 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 37: \\( 37 \\div 3 = 12 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 38: \\( 38 \\div 3 = 12 \\) (remainder 2)\n- 39: \\( 39 \\div 3 = 13 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 40: \\( 40 \\div 3 = 13 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 41: \\( 41 \\div 3 = 13 \\) (remainder 2)\n- 42: \\( 42 \\div 3 = 14 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 43: \\( 43 \\div 3 = 14 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 44: \\( 44 \\div 3 = 14 \\) (remainder 2)\n- 45: \\( 45 \\div 3 = 15 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 46: \\( 46 \\div 3 = 15 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 47: \\( 47 \\div 3 = 15 \\) (remainder 2)\n- 48: \\( 48 \\div 3 = 16 \\) (remainder 0)\n- 49: \\( 49 \\div 3 = 16 \\) (remainder 1)\n- 50: \\( 50 \\div 3 = 16 \\) (remainder 2)\n\nThe numbers that are not divisible by 3 are 31, 34, 37, 40, 43, 46, and 49. Therefore, there are 7 such numbers.\n\nThus, the number of students who showed up for the archery class this week is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
