        {
          "problem": "Fake gold bricks are made by covering concrete cubes with gold paint, so the cost of the paint is proportional to their surface area while the cost of the concrete is proportional to their volume.  If a 1 inch cube costs $\\$1.30$ to make while a 2 inch cube costs $\\$6.80$, then how much would a 3 inch cube cost?",
          "solution": "Let $x$ be the cost per square inch of gold paint, and let $y$ be the cost per cubic inch of concrete.  Since a 1 inch cube has surface area 6 $\\text{in}^2$ and volume 1 $\\text{in}^3$ its total price will be $6x+y$ dollars.  Similarly, a 2 inch cube has surface area 24 $\\text{in}^2$ and volume 8 $\\text{in}^3$, so its total price will be $24x+8y$ dollars.  We are given that \\begin{align*} 6x+y &=\\$1.30 \\\\ 24x+8y&= \\$6.80 \\end{align*} Subtracting 4 times the first equation from the second gives $4y=\\$1.60$, so $y=\\$0.40$.  Hence $6x=\\$0.90$, so $x=\\$0.15$.  Since a 3 inch cube has surface area 54 $\\text{in}^2$ and volume 27 $\\text{in}^3$, its total price will be a total of $54(\\$0.15)+27(\\$0.40)=\\boxed{\\$18.90}$.",
          "answer": "\\$18.90",
          "subject": "Algebra",
          "level": 5,
          "unique_id": "test/algebra/2780.json",
          "model_output": "Let's denote the cost of the paint as $C$ and the cost of the concrete as $V$. We are given that the cost of the paint is proportional to the surface area of the cube, and the cost of the concrete is proportional to the volume of the cube.\n\nFor a 1 inch cube, the surface area is $6 \\times 1^2 = 6$ square inches, and the cost of the paint is $\\$1.30$. For a 2 inch cube, the surface area is $6 \\times 2^2 = 24$ square inches, and the cost of the paint is $\\$6.80$.\n\nWe can set up a proportion to relate the cost of the paint to the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\\frac{C}{6} = \\frac{\\$1.30}{6 \\text{ square inches}}$$\n\nFor a 3 inch cube, the surface area is $6 \\times 3^2 = 54$ square inches. We can set up another proportion to relate the cost of the paint to the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\\frac{C}{6} = \\frac{\\$1.30}{6 \\text{ square inches}}$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $C$ (the cost of the paint) using the second proportion:\n\n$$C = \\frac{\\$1.30}{6} \\times 6 \\text{ square inches} = \\$1.30 \\times 1 \\text{ square inch} = \\$1.30$$\n\nNow that we have the cost of the paint, we can find the cost of the concrete for a 3 inch cube. Since the cost of the concrete is proportional to the volume of the cube, we can set up a proportion to relate the cost of the concrete to the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\\frac{V}{6 \\text{ cubic inches}} = \\frac{\\$1.30}{6 \\text{ square inches}}$$\n\nFor a 3 inch cube, the volume is $6 \\times 3^3 = 162$ cubic inches. We can set up another proportion to relate the cost of the concrete to the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\\frac{V}{6 \\text{ cubic inches}} = \\frac{\\$1.30}{6 \\text{ square inches}}$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $V$ (the cost of the concrete):\n\n$$V = \\frac{\\$1.30}{6} \\times 6 \\text{ cubic inches} = \\$1.30 \\times 3 \\text{ cubic inches} = \\$3.90$$\n\nTherefore, the cost of the concrete for a 3 inch cube is $\\boxed{\\$3.90}$.",
