          "model_output": "Given the equations:\n1. $\\log_6 (x-y) + \\log_6 (x+y) = 2$\n2. $\\log_y 5x = 2$\n\nFrom the second equation, we have:\n\\[ \\log_y 5x = 2 \\]\nThis implies:\n\\[ y^2 = 5x \\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we can rewrite it as:\n\\[ \\log_6 (x-y) + \\log_6 (x+y) = 2 \\]\nUsing the property of logarithms that $\\log_a b + \\log_a c = \\log_a (bc)$, we get:\n\\[ \\log_6 ((x-y)(x+y)) = 2 \\]\n\\[ \\log_6 (x^2 - y^2) = 2 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = 6^2 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = 36 \\]\n\nNow, we have two equations:\n1. $y^2 = 5x$\n2. $x^2 - y^2 = 36$\n\nSubstitute $y^2 = 5x$ into the second equation:\n\\[ x^2 - 5x = 36 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 5x - 36 = 0 \\]\n\nSolve the quadratic equation:\n\\[ x = \\frac{-(-5) \\pm \\sqrt{(-5)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-36)}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 144}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{169}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{5 \\pm 13}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two possible values for $x$:\n\\[ x = \\frac{5 + 13}{2} = 9 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{5 - 13}{2} = -4 \\]\n\nSince $x$ must be positive in the context of logarithms, we discard the negative solution. Therefore, the value of $x$ is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
