l equal to the sum of the second main diagonal:\n\\[\n4n - 8 = (n+2) + (n-1) + (n+2) + (n-1)\n\\]\n\n3. Simplifying the right side of the equation:\n\\[\n4n - 8 = 4n + 2\n\\]\n\n4. Subtracting $4n$ from both sides:\n\\[\n-8 = 2\n\\]\n\nThis equation is also not true, which means our initial assumption about the sum of the numbers in the first main diagonal being equal to the sum of the second main diagonal is incorrect. However, we can still find the value of $n$ by considering the sum of the numbers in the second main diagonal:\n\n1. The second main diagonal contains the numbers $3$, $n+2$, $n-1$, and $2n-9$. The sum of these numbers is:\n\\[\n3 + (n+2) + (n-1) + (2n-9) = 4n - 5\n\\]\n\n2. Since the sum of the numbers in each diagonal must be equal, we set the sum of the second main diagonal equal to the sum of the first main diagonal:\n\\[\n4n - 5 = (n-3) + (n+1) + (n-1) + (2n-9)\n\\]\n\n3. Simplifying the right side of the equation:\n\\[\n4n - 5 = 4n - 8\n\\]\n\n4. Subtracting $4n$ from both sides:\n\\[\n-5 = -8\n\\]\n\nThis equation is also not true, which means our initial assumption about the sum of the numbers in the second main diagonal being equal to the sum of the first main diagonal is incorrect. However, we can still find the value of $n$ by considering the sum of the numbers in the third row:\n\n1. The third row contains the numbers $n+2$, $n-1$, and $2n-9$. The sum of these numbers is:\n\\[\n(n+2) + (n-1) + (2n-9) = 4n - 8\n\\]\n\n2. Since the sum of the numbers in each row must be equal, we set the sum of the third row equal to the sum of the first row:\n\\[\n4n - 8 = 4n - 8\n\\]\n\n3. This equation is true for any value of $n$, which means our initial assumption about the sum of the numbers in the third row being equal to the sum of the first row is correct.\n\nTherefore, the value of $n$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{n = 2}\n\\]",
